    tagline:"Gestational diabetes does not announce itself. It arrives silently, reshaping the metabolic landscape of pregnancy so gradually that by the time blood glucose is measurably elevated, the biological disruption has been building for months.",
    story:"Akpene Torgbe is a second-year midwifery student on her first antenatal clinic placement at a regional hospital in the Volta Region.\n\nA 31-year-old woman — her third pregnancy — arrives for her booking visit at 10 weeks. She is visibly overweight, her blood pressure is borderline, and she mentions her second child weighed 4.2kg at birth.\n\nThe midwife supervising Akpene marks several items on the risk assessment form: BMI >30, previous macrosomic baby, family history of diabetes (mentioned in passing — her mother was on tablets for 'sugar').\n\n\"Three risk factors,\" the midwife says quietly to Akpene. \"What does that mean for this woman?\"\n\nAkpene thinks. \"She needs early glucose testing?\"\n\n\"She needs more than that. She needs us to understand WHY pregnancy creates diabetes in some women — because if we understand the mechanism, we understand why it starts when it does, why some women are more vulnerable, and why even a woman with a normal fasting glucose in the first trimester can develop dangerous hyperglycaemia by the third.\"\n\nShe turns to the patient and smiles warmly, then leads Akpene to a quiet corner.\n\n\"Pregnancy is a state of progressive insulin resistance — by design. The placenta produces hormones — human placental lactogen, progesterone, cortisol, prolactin — that deliberately antagonise insulin action, redirecting glucose to the fetus. In most women, the pancreatic beta cells compensate by producing more insulin. But in women whose beta cell reserve is already limited — by genetics, by obesity, by previous metabolic stress — that compensation fails. The glucose that should have been redirected to the fetus instead accumulates in the maternal bloodstream.\"\n\nShe pauses.\n\n\"This woman's previous macrosomic baby is not a coincidence. It is a warning signal that her beta cell reserve was insufficient in that pregnancy too. We just didn't diagnose it.\"\n\nAkpene looks at the risk assessment form differently. The numbers are not just boxes to tick. They are a biological history.\n\nThis module is that biology.",
