        h:"🔬 Normal Glucose Metabolism in Pregnancy — The Baseline",
        a:"To understand gestational diabetes, you must first understand what normal glucose metabolism in pregnancy looks like — because GDM is not simply 'diabetes that happens during pregnancy.' It is the pathological failure of a specific, normal, and physiologically necessary metabolic adaptation that every pregnant woman undergoes.",
        c:"NORMAL METABOLIC ADAPTATION TO PREGNANCY:\nEARLY PREGNANCY (first trimester): anabolic phase — enhanced insulin sensitivity, increased glucose uptake, fat deposition building maternal energy stores for later demands. Fasting glucose falls slightly in normal early pregnancy.\nMID-TO-LATE PREGNANCY (second and third trimesters): progressive INSULIN RESISTANCE — a physiologically normal state driven by placental hormones, designed to ensure a continuous glucose supply to the rapidly growing fetus by reducing maternal peripheral glucose uptake\nKEY INSULIN-ANTAGONISING PLACENTAL HORMONES:\nHUMAN PLACENTAL LACTOGEN (hPL): the dominant contributor — concentration rises continuously through pregnancy, correlating with placental growth. Directly antagonises insulin signalling at peripheral tissues (muscle, adipose). By term, hPL-driven insulin resistance requires 2-3 times the normal insulin secretion to maintain euglycaemia.\nPROGESTERONE: reduces insulin receptor sensitivity and impairs glucose-stimulated insulin secretion\nCORTISOL: rises progressively in pregnancy — stimulates hepatic gluconeogenesis and reduces insulin-mediated glucose uptake\nPROLACTIN AND PLACENTAL GROWTH HORMONE: additional contributors to insulin resistance and altered glucose homeostasis\n\nBETA CELL COMPENSATION — THE CRITICAL VARIABLE:\nIn women with adequate pancreatic beta cell reserve, progressive insulin resistance is matched by proportional increase in insulin secretion — glucose remains in the normal range throughout pregnancy. This physiological compensation is what the majority of pregnant women achieve.\nThe pancreatic beta cell hypertrophy and hyperplasia that normally occurs in pregnancy represents the body's attempt to match insulin secretion to rising resistance — this adaptation fails in women who develop GDM\n\nTHE GDM FAILURE POINT:\nGDM occurs when beta cell compensation is INSUFFICIENT to overcome placental insulin resistance — plasma glucose rises above the defined diagnostic thresholds\nGDM is therefore a state of RELATIVE insulin deficiency superimposed on physiological insulin resistance — NOT simply pre-existing type 2 diabetes unmasked by pregnancy (though some GDM cases do represent pre-existing undiagnosed T2DM)\nPEAK RISK PERIOD: second half of pregnancy, when placental hormone production and insulin resistance are at their greatest — which is why GDM screening is timed to 24-28 weeks gestation in most guidelines, not the first trimester",
