        a:"Gestational diabetes is not distributed equally across populations — and understanding why specific populations carry higher risk is not merely an academic exercise. It directly determines screening strategy, alert thresholds, and the intensity of preventive and therapeutic intervention appropriate for a given clinical setting.",
        c:"ESTABLISHED RISK FACTORS FOR GDM:\nNON-MODIFIABLE: previous GDM (recurrence risk 30-70% in subsequent pregnancies), previous macrosomic baby (birthweight >4kg), family history of type 2 diabetes in first-degree relative, polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS — associated with underlying insulin resistance), advancing maternal age (>35 years)\nMODIFIABLE: pre-pregnancy overweight and obesity (BMI ≥25 or ≥30) — the strongest modifiable risk factor globally; excessive gestational weight gain; sedentary lifestyle; multiparity\nETHNIC ORIGIN: South Asian, Middle Eastern, Black African, Afro-Caribbean, East Asian women all carry higher GDM risk than white European women at equivalent BMI — a risk difference that persists after adjustment for body weight and other factors, suggesting inherent differences in beta cell reserve or insulin resistance susceptibility across ancestral populations\n\nGDM EPIDEMIOLOGY IN GHANA AND WEST AFRICA:\nGlobal GDM prevalence (IDF 2021): approximately 16% of live births worldwide affected by hyperglycaemia in pregnancy\nWest Africa specific: studies from Ghana, Nigeria, and Senegal report GDM prevalence of 5-14% depending on diagnostic criteria used, population studied, and screening approach — rates that are rising in parallel with the regional obesity epidemic and urbanisation-associated lifestyle changes\nGHANA-SPECIFIC BURDEN: a systematic review of GDM in sub-Saharan Africa (Mwanri et al.) found prevalence rates of 6-14% in West African populations using IADPSG criteria — substantially higher than often assumed, and likely underestimated given the limited systematic screening in many settings\nURBANISATION AND DIETARY TRANSITION: the rapid shift from traditional high-fibre, lower-glycaemic-index diets to refined carbohydrate-rich urban diets is driving GDM risk increases across West African cities — creating a population-level metabolic transition with significant maternal and neonatal health implications\n\nWHY ETHNICITY IS A RISK FACTOR — THE BIOLOGICAL BASIS:\nStudies have consistently shown that Black African and South Asian women develop GDM at LOWER BMI thresholds than white European women — suggesting either reduced beta cell secretory capacity at equivalent insulin resistance levels, or greater insulin resistance for a given body weight\nThis has led to proposals (adopted in some Asian countries) for ethnicity-adjusted BMI thresholds for obesity classification and GDM risk stratification — though these adjustments are not yet universally implemented in international guidelines\nPRACTICAL IMPLICATION FOR GHANAIAN CLINICAL PRACTICE: using BMI thresholds calibrated on European populations may systematically underestimate GDM risk in Ghanaian women — arguing for a lower threshold of suspicion and more proactive screening than global guidelines designed for predominantly European populations might suggest",
